1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:05,540
Can we follow a game's target while turning the whole view less?

2
00:00:05,780 --> 00:00:07,700
We will examine aiming and viewing in a cleaning game,

3
00:00:07,700 --> 00:00:09,360
then, after the view changes in a puzzle game,

4
00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:12,120
the cues for recognizing a target.

5
00:00:12,380 --> 00:00:14,900
We will connect these observations to motion-sickness-aware camera choices

6
00:00:14,900 --> 00:00:17,360
and a way to restore the original settings.

7
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:20,120
First, as water removes dirt from the floor,

8
00:00:20,120 --> 00:00:22,500
watch the red line marking the spray direction

9
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:24,400
and the blue marker on the background post separately.

10
00:00:27,117 --> 00:00:29,867
This is a PowerWash Simulator development demonstration.

11
00:00:30,077 --> 00:00:33,547
Watch the cleaning tool and the playground separately.

12
00:00:33,777 --> 00:00:36,927
As the aim sweeps across the floor, the background moves too.

13
00:00:37,157 --> 00:00:38,987
The next shot behaves differently.

14
00:00:39,137 --> 00:00:41,852
Posts and floor boundaries stay in roughly the same screen

15
00:00:41,868 --> 00:00:45,687
positions, while the nozzle and aiming point move within the image.

16
00:00:45,837 --> 00:00:48,247
The developer calls this Aim Mode.

17
00:00:48,337 --> 00:00:51,364
Separating the viewing direction from tool aiming reduces the

18
00:00:51,380 --> 00:00:54,324
need to turn the whole image while following a small target.

19
00:00:54,340 --> 00:00:57,467
Watch how tool aiming connects to background movement.

20
00:00:57,757 --> 00:01:01,107
The same task can involve different amounts of whole-view rotation.

21
00:01:01,457 --> 00:01:03,967
This footage shows a development version from 2022.

22
00:01:04,277 --> 00:01:07,607
It is not a comparison of every current setting or individual motion-sickness responses.

23
00:01:07,877 --> 00:01:10,904
We will separate necessary view movement from aiming, and examine

24
00:01:10,920 --> 00:01:13,647
camera design that gives players control over that relationship.

25
00:01:20,717 --> 00:01:25,847
The game image can travel and turn while the player remains seated.

26
00:01:26,177 --> 00:01:31,187
Conflict between visual and bodily motion signals is one explanatory theory of motion sickness.

27
00:01:31,437 --> 00:01:34,987
People do not all respond in the same way to a moving camera.

28
00:01:35,197 --> 00:01:39,087
Someone else being comfortable does not make your discomfort disappear.

29
00:01:39,277 --> 00:01:42,887
Our focus is a developer choice, not treatment.

30
00:01:42,997 --> 00:01:49,524
Separating movement needed for control from movement added for presentation helps identify what players could adjust.

31
00:01:49,540 --> 00:01:55,367
Think of the left side as turning toward a target, and the right side as extra shake layered on top.

32
00:01:55,557 --> 00:01:58,957
The first design question is whether these should be controlled separately.

33
00:01:59,683 --> 00:02:02,773
Return to the Aim Mode demonstration.

34
00:02:02,963 --> 00:02:06,450
The nozzle moves toward the left and right of the image,

35
00:02:06,466 --> 00:02:09,373
while the stairs and trees remain in place for a time.

36
00:02:09,543 --> 00:02:12,130
The aiming position on the floor is

37
00:02:12,146 --> 00:02:14,693
separated from the direction of the view.

38
00:02:14,803 --> 00:02:18,613
Moving the tool and rotating the whole image are different events.

39
00:02:18,783 --> 00:02:21,010
At the edge, the view turns and

40
00:02:21,026 --> 00:02:23,200
the roundabout comes into view.

41
00:02:23,216 --> 00:02:25,250
Aim Mode does not mean that the camera

42
00:02:25,266 --> 00:02:27,033
is completely fixed at all times.

43
00:02:27,223 --> 00:02:31,250
Now watch the roundabout being cleaned. Follow the surface

44
00:02:31,266 --> 00:02:34,910
reached by the spray, rather than the distant trees. The

45
00:02:34,926 --> 00:02:37,793
task continues during stretches with less view rotation.

46
00:02:38,383 --> 00:02:41,713
Separating aim still requires a way to look elsewhere.

47
00:02:41,903 --> 00:02:44,490
Designers need to consider how aiming toward an

48
00:02:44,506 --> 00:02:47,053
edge connects to looking around the environment.

49
00:02:47,263 --> 00:02:50,853
The demonstration shows how a task connects to a viewpoint.

50
00:02:50,983 --> 00:02:53,470
It does not show that the option is

51
00:02:53,486 --> 00:02:55,373
equally comfortable for everyone.

52
00:03:02,250 --> 00:03:05,800
Removing all camera movement would prevent looking elsewhere.

53
00:03:05,930 --> 00:03:12,600
Instead, consider view-turning input, tool aiming and walking bob as separate layers.

54
00:03:12,870 --> 00:03:16,877
Vertical bob added to suggest walking and shake added to emphasize

55
00:03:16,893 --> 00:03:20,980
impact do not serve the same purpose as facing a target.

56
00:03:21,070 --> 00:03:22,740
Their adjustment controls can be separate too.

57
00:03:22,830 --> 00:03:27,540
Accessibility guidance recommends player control over these additional movements.

58
00:03:27,750 --> 00:03:32,267
Having an option and achieving comfort for an individual are separate questions.

59
00:03:32,283 --> 00:03:38,357
In this diagram, view rotation remains available while extra shake is reduced. In implementation,

60
00:03:38,373 --> 00:03:44,060
separate controls by responsibility so one switch does not unexpectedly change tool aiming or movement.

61
00:03:44,783 --> 00:03:47,813
This is another part of the playground task.

62
00:03:47,943 --> 00:03:50,570
When the player moves sideways between posts, the positions

63
00:03:50,586 --> 00:03:53,533
of nearby posts and distant trees change together.

64
00:03:53,783 --> 00:03:56,833
If the surface is hidden, the viewing position may need to change.

65
00:03:57,163 --> 00:03:59,870
Removing that movement and separating aim

66
00:03:59,886 --> 00:04:02,433
after taking a position are different choices.

67
00:04:02,563 --> 00:04:04,950
In the upper-structure shot, the view tilts

68
00:04:04,966 --> 00:04:07,793
upward and the spray reaches a higher surface.

69
00:04:07,883 --> 00:04:11,753
A target moving from the floor to overhead geometry can require a different viewing direction.

70
00:04:11,773 --> 00:04:14,430
On another surface, the nozzle follows

71
00:04:14,446 --> 00:04:16,593
posts and the broad surfaces beside them.

72
00:04:16,983 --> 00:04:19,970
Distinguish taking a position from cleaning a

73
00:04:19,986 --> 00:04:22,153
surface while facing roughly the same direction.

74
00:04:22,543 --> 00:04:24,830
Even if reducing screen movement is a goal,

75
00:04:24,846 --> 00:04:26,973
hidden surfaces must remain discoverable.

76
00:04:27,303 --> 00:04:30,010
An aiming option can provide another way to perform

77
00:04:30,026 --> 00:04:32,393
the task rather than remove the task itself.

78
00:04:32,683 --> 00:04:34,933
These are excerpts from different moments.

79
00:04:35,383 --> 00:04:38,010
They are not a controlled repetition of identical inputs, so begin

80
00:04:38,026 --> 00:04:40,473
by describing the visible relationship between movement and aim.

81
00:04:47,233 --> 00:04:54,463
A single menu item called screen movement makes it hard to know what changing it does.

82
00:04:54,613 --> 00:04:58,723
Explain view sensitivity, tool aiming and extra shake separately.

83
00:04:58,973 --> 00:05:02,703
Sensitivity concerns how much the view turns for an input.

84
00:05:02,813 --> 00:05:06,343
Aim Mode concerns the relationship between aim and view.

85
00:05:06,553 --> 00:05:08,483
Those terms are not interchangeable.

86
00:05:08,713 --> 00:05:11,363
This option list is a design proposal.

87
00:05:11,373 --> 00:05:14,990
It does not mean the development demonstration contains this exact menu.

88
00:05:15,006 --> 00:05:18,463
Distinguish the actual game from our proposed interface.

89
00:05:18,853 --> 00:05:24,623
A short explanation and a reset let players inspect a difference and return to the original value.

90
00:05:24,953 --> 00:05:30,083
Explaining the choice comes before choosing one recommended setting for everyone.

91
00:05:31,117 --> 00:05:33,417
Even with separate aiming, after looking in another direction,

92
00:05:33,417 --> 00:05:35,817
we must be able to recognize the target again.

93
00:05:36,137 --> 00:05:38,177
In the next trailer’s separate excerpts,

94
00:05:38,177 --> 00:05:42,077
we will examine how walls and floors help us recognize a target.

95
00:05:42,800 --> 00:05:46,670
This is official gameplay footage of The Talos Principle 2.

96
00:05:46,840 --> 00:05:49,007
Watch platform movement, then the view

97
00:05:49,023 --> 00:05:51,270
turning toward overhead geometry.

98
00:05:51,440 --> 00:05:54,610
In another shot, walls and lasers tilt.

99
00:05:54,720 --> 00:05:57,450
The orientation of the puzzle view changes.

100
00:05:57,660 --> 00:06:00,067
Changing space or looking in a new

101
00:06:00,083 --> 00:06:02,770
direction can be connected to game rules.

102
00:06:02,880 --> 00:06:05,810
These separate shots are not continuous play.

103
00:06:06,020 --> 00:06:09,090
The important question is whether the new orientation remains understandable.

104
00:06:09,520 --> 00:06:11,757
Return to a different surface in the cleaning game.

105
00:06:11,773 --> 00:06:15,827
When looking up beneath the structure and then toward another face,

106
00:06:15,843 --> 00:06:20,030
nearby planks and posts help identify the viewing direction after a turn.

107
00:06:20,320 --> 00:06:23,130
Here, the view changes to find a target.

108
00:06:23,260 --> 00:06:26,627
It is not the same mechanic as the puzzle, but both raise the

109
00:06:26,643 --> 00:06:29,450
question of reading where the view is facing after a change.

110
00:06:29,680 --> 00:06:33,430
This footage alone cannot establish that a particular transition is comfortable.

111
00:06:33,720 --> 00:06:36,107
Observe the orientation change and the remaining cues before

112
00:06:36,123 --> 00:06:39,225
considering choices in how the transition is presented.

113
00:06:45,950 --> 00:06:50,000
A gradual transition can allow time to follow the movement.

114
00:06:50,210 --> 00:06:54,120
A longer-moving image can also be less comfortable for someone else.

115
00:06:54,330 --> 00:06:56,840
One transition cannot represent everyone.

116
00:06:57,230 --> 00:07:01,960
An immediate change shortens the moving interval but may make the arrival orientation harder to read.

117
00:07:02,090 --> 00:07:05,620
Motion duration and spatial understanding are separate questions.

118
00:07:05,770 --> 00:07:08,040
These two paths are explanatory diagrams.

119
00:07:08,190 --> 00:07:12,847
They do not claim that the game footage we just watched implements these transition options.

120
00:07:12,863 --> 00:07:17,420
Consider both a choice to reduce transition motion and cues for reading the arrival direction.

121
00:07:17,630 --> 00:07:23,950
When checking comfort, change one effect at a time and allow players to stop or revert immediately.

122
00:07:24,667 --> 00:07:26,897
This is another cleaning interval around the slide.

123
00:07:27,247 --> 00:07:29,294
Can you find the surface reached

124
00:07:29,310 --> 00:07:31,037
by the spray while the view turns?

125
00:07:31,387 --> 00:07:35,337
The floor, side surface and nearby structure occupy different positions.

126
00:07:35,927 --> 00:07:39,477
When a new area comes into view, background lines move too.

127
00:07:40,047 --> 00:07:42,894
After the view settles, the relationship between the spray

128
00:07:42,910 --> 00:07:45,017
and the surface being cleaned should be readable again.

129
00:07:45,427 --> 00:07:47,517
Now look at the nearby planks and posts.

130
00:07:47,947 --> 00:07:50,954
Understanding the task requires seeing which surface the

131
00:07:50,970 --> 00:07:53,994
tool points at, as well as seeing the nozzle itself.

132
00:07:54,010 --> 00:07:57,857
That relationship should remain after changing the aiming mode.

133
00:07:58,207 --> 00:08:01,574
Reducing camera motion while obscuring the target or confusing

134
00:08:01,590 --> 00:08:04,477
the aiming position can introduce another control problem.

135
00:08:04,727 --> 00:08:06,454
What these shots directly show is the

136
00:08:06,470 --> 00:08:09,277
position of tool, surface and view.

137
00:08:09,567 --> 00:08:13,097
Do not confuse them with measurements of a player’s symptoms or comfort.

138
00:08:13,347 --> 00:08:15,817
Make the review questions specific.

139
00:08:16,187 --> 00:08:19,794
Beyond asking whether the image moves less, ask whether the cleaning target is

140
00:08:19,810 --> 00:08:22,667
visible and whether aiming remains readable after moving to another surface.

141
00:08:29,400 --> 00:08:32,790
An option is less useful when it is hard to find.

142
00:08:33,060 --> 00:08:36,647
Make camera and aiming choices recognizable in

143
00:08:36,663 --> 00:08:39,570
initial settings and accessible again during play.

144
00:08:39,820 --> 00:08:43,090
Explain both what changes and what remains available.

145
00:08:43,340 --> 00:08:47,367
For example, treating tool aiming separately from view rotation

146
00:08:47,383 --> 00:08:50,990
still leaves the ability to look in another direction available.

147
00:08:51,240 --> 00:08:53,890
Check saving and restoring defaults in practice.

148
00:08:54,180 --> 00:09:00,550
If values change after restarting or switching input devices, players may have to find the same choice repeatedly.

149
00:09:00,960 --> 00:09:03,650
These checks verify how the camera options operate.

150
00:09:03,920 --> 00:09:10,250
Comfort requires separate individual feedback; testing should not become an exercise in enduring discomfort.

151
00:09:11,250 --> 00:09:14,620
Finally, watch another face of the playground structure being cleaned.

152
00:09:14,870 --> 00:09:17,617
While following a nearby surface, first

153
00:09:17,633 --> 00:09:20,200
identify where the nozzle and spray point.

154
00:09:20,230 --> 00:09:24,640
Moving around after cleaning part of a surface changes the arrangement of posts and planks.

155
00:09:24,950 --> 00:09:27,360
Repositioning reveals another face; aiming

156
00:09:27,650 --> 00:09:30,320
then continues the task on that face.

157
00:09:30,690 --> 00:09:33,860
In the shot aimed at an overhead crossbar, the camera looks upward too.

158
00:09:34,030 --> 00:09:36,797
Distinguish moments when the whole background moves

159
00:09:36,813 --> 00:09:39,077
from moments when the tool moves around a target.

160
00:09:39,093 --> 00:09:41,937
The Aim Mode idea was to separate aiming from

161
00:09:41,953 --> 00:09:44,440
viewing direction during this kind of task.

162
00:09:44,930 --> 00:09:47,137
It does not mean eliminating every

163
00:09:47,153 --> 00:09:49,160
movement or everyone’s discomfort.

164
00:09:49,530 --> 00:09:51,417
When reviewing an example, first

165
00:09:51,433 --> 00:09:53,480
record what moves in each shot.

166
00:09:53,870 --> 00:09:57,257
Distinguishing necessary travel, aiming changes and added

167
00:09:57,273 --> 00:10:00,140
presentation makes the available choices more concrete.

168
00:10:00,530 --> 00:10:03,887
Here we observed an actual cleaning task continuing across different views.

169
00:10:03,903 --> 00:10:07,797
As a developer, you can now ask whether the same

170
00:10:07,813 --> 00:10:10,420
target remains readable with another control method.

171
00:10:17,400 --> 00:10:20,550
The point is not to stop the camera unconditionally.

172
00:10:20,740 --> 00:10:27,210
Separate necessary viewpoint movement, tool aiming and added presentation, then identify what players can control.

173
00:10:27,380 --> 00:10:33,250
In actual game shots, we examined what moves; in the diagrams, what responsibilities could be separated.

174
00:10:33,520 --> 00:10:37,770
Check that the target and current orientation remain readable after changing an option.

175
00:10:38,060 --> 00:10:41,207
Games require different controls, and people respond differently.

176
00:10:41,223 --> 00:10:44,987
Instead of promising one correct value, provide understandable

177
00:10:45,003 --> 00:10:48,790
choices, a way to revert and feedback from actual play.

178
00:10:49,040 --> 00:10:53,050
In your own game, start by separating the responsibilities of the camera and aiming.

179
00:10:53,340 --> 00:10:59,160
To learn this kind of implementation together, see the programming coaching link in the description and ending.
